Resume the transmitter when data returns after a starve mute

patches/0015 mutes the transmitter when no DMA block has arrived for
tx_starve_timeout_ms, and it was one-way: later blocks re-armed the timer and
did nothing else. Only a fresh buffer enable unmuted.

That mute also hands the DAC to the DDS, and from then on nothing drains the
DMA. So it was worse than "stays quiet": the blocks a program submits sit in
the queue, its writes time out, and iiod refuses a new buffer as busy while the
old one cannot be torn down. Closing the connection was the only way back.

Reported from SDR Console (Windows, the stock Pluto driver), which keeps one
transmit buffer open and stops feeding it between transmissions: "TX only
starts once after connecting ... the USER LED still lights up, but nothing is
output. Only after a stop and a new connection can I send exactly once again."
The LED is patches/0012 following the attenuator the program had just written,
with the TX LO still powered down and the DAC still on the DDS.

Measured on the 6.12 kernel (firmware-modern), on an R1 (v2.4) and an R2 (v0.2)
over libiio USB, with
tools/tx-idle-cases/resume-after-starve.py - zero samples at -89.5 dB, so no
output is commanded - before this patch, identically on both:

  feeding                      -89.50 / -89.50  LO_pd=0  source=DMA
  paused 1.0 s                 -89.75 / -89.75  LO_pd=1  source=DDS
  pushing into the same buffer FAILED: [Errno 110]
  feeding again, same buffer   -89.75 / -89.75  LO_pd=1  source=DDS
  a new buffer was REFUSED for 10.0 s: [Errno 16] Resource busy

and after it:

  feeding                      -89.50 / -89.50  LO_pd=0  source=DMA
  paused 1.0 s                 -89.75 / -89.75  LO_pd=1  source=DDS
  feeding again, same buffer   -89.50 / -89.50  LO_pd=0  source=DMA
  paused again                 -89.75 / -89.75  LO_pd=1  source=DDS
  feeding a third time         -89.50 / -89.50  LO_pd=0  source=DMA

with the kernel saying so each time:

  no transmit data for 250 ms - muting the transmitter
  transmit data is back - unmuting the transmitter

What changes:

  - the watchdog records that it was the one that muted (tx_starved);
  - a block arriving in that state schedules a work item that re-selects the
    DMA source for the enabled channels and then calls
    cf_axi_dds_tx_rf_mute(false), the order and the call a buffer enable uses;
  - postdisable cancels that work before it mutes, and a mutex keeps the
    watchdog's mute and the resume from interleaving.

What does not: a stalled or killed client is muted after the same 250 ms, and
again after every later stall, because the block that triggers the resume has
already re-armed the timer. Because the unmute is the enable's own call, the
same rules decide what comes back: the cached attenuation only when both
channels still read muted (0005), never zero (0019), nothing while tx_disable
is set (0016) or the die is over tx_temp_limit (0018). Cyclic streams are
untouched: no further block ever arrives on one.

This retires the "fires once and does not re-arm" limit recorded in
IDLE-CASES.md and docs/transmitter-safety.md.

On this 5.15 tree the change is the same code, and is COMPILED ONLY: both
boards on the bench run the 6.12 kernel, so it has not been run here.

diff --git a/linux/drivers/iio/frequency/cf_axi_dds.c b/linux/drivers/iio/frequency/cf_axi_dds.c
index bdda4987a..4f629dbce 100755
--- a/linux/drivers/iio/frequency/cf_axi_dds.c
+++ b/linux/drivers/iio/frequency/cf_axi_dds.c
@@ -148,6 +148,17 @@ struct cf_axi_dds_state {
 	unsigned int			tx_cyclic_ms;
 	bool				tx_cyclic;
 	unsigned int			tx_starve_armed_ms;	/* the wait in force, for the log */
+	/*
+	 * Set when it was the watchdog that muted, with the buffer still
+	 * enabled. Data arriving again then puts the transmitter back the way a
+	 * buffer enable would; without this the mute was one-way, and a program
+	 * that keeps one buffer open between transmissions could transmit once.
+	 * tx_mute_lock keeps the watchdog's mute and that resume from
+	 * interleaving.
+	 */
+	bool				tx_starved;
+	struct work_struct		tx_resume_work;
+	struct mutex			tx_mute_lock;
 	/*
 	 * The DAC core's VDMA status bits are STICKY and read-to-clear, so
 	 * they answer "did it happen" and not "how often". Sampling them once
@@ -1385,8 +1396,12 @@ static void cf_axi_dds_tx_starve_fn(struct work_struct *work)
 						   struct cf_axi_dds_state,
 						   tx_starve_work);
 
-	if (st->tx_muted)
+	mutex_lock(&st->tx_mute_lock);
+
+	if (st->tx_muted) {
+		mutex_unlock(&st->tx_mute_lock);
 		return;
+	}
 
 	dev_warn(&st->indio_dev->dev,
 		 "%s for %u ms - muting the transmitter\n",
@@ -1396,6 +1411,61 @@ static void cf_axi_dds_tx_starve_fn(struct work_struct *work)
 
 	cf_axi_dds_tx_rf_mute(st, true);
 	cf_axi_dds_datasel(st, -1, DATA_SEL_DDS);
+	st->tx_starved = true;
+
+	mutex_unlock(&st->tx_mute_lock);
+}
+
+static int cf_axi_dds_update_scan_mode(struct iio_dev *indio_dev,
+	const unsigned long *scan_mask);
+
+/*
+ * Data is arriving again on a buffer the watchdog muted.
+ *
+ * The mute above also hands the DAC to the DDS, and from then on nothing
+ * drains the DMA: the blocks a program submits sit in the queue, its writes
+ * time out, and iiod refuses a new buffer as busy while the old one cannot be
+ * torn down. Only closing the connection got the transmitter back. Measured
+ * on both boards with tools/tx-idle-cases/resume-after-starve.py, and
+ * reported from SDR Console, which keeps one buffer open and stops feeding it
+ * between transmissions: "TX works once per connection".
+ *
+ * So a block arriving after a starve mute undoes exactly what the mute did,
+ * in the order a buffer enable uses: the data path first, the attenuation
+ * last. cf_axi_dds_tx_rf_mute(false) is the same call the enable makes, so
+ * the same rules decide what comes back - the cached attenuation only if both
+ * channels still read muted, tx_disable and the temperature limit still
+ * refuse - and the block that got us here has already re-armed the watchdog,
+ * so a second stall mutes again.
+ *
+ * A work item because unmuting talks to the AD9361 over SPI, and that sleeps.
+ */
+static void cf_axi_dds_tx_resume_fn(struct work_struct *work)
+{
+	struct cf_axi_dds_state *st = container_of(work,
+						   struct cf_axi_dds_state,
+						   tx_resume_work);
+	struct iio_dev *indio_dev = st->indio_dev;
+
+	mutex_lock(&st->tx_mute_lock);
+
+	/*
+	 * postdisable cancels this work before it mutes, so a buffer on its way
+	 * down is still enabled here and is muted again straight after.
+	 */
+	if (st->tx_starved && iio_buffer_enabled(indio_dev) &&
+	    indio_dev->active_scan_mask) {
+		st->tx_starved = false;
+
+		dev_info(&indio_dev->dev,
+			 "transmit data is back - unmuting the transmitter\n");
+
+		cf_axi_dds_update_scan_mode(indio_dev,
+					    indio_dev->active_scan_mask);
+		cf_axi_dds_tx_rf_mute(st, false);
+	}
+
+	mutex_unlock(&st->tx_mute_lock);
 }
 
 /*
@@ -1419,6 +1489,19 @@ void cf_axi_dds_tx_starve_arm(struct cf_axi_dds_state *st, bool cyclic)
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(cf_axi_dds_tx_starve_arm);
 
+/*
+ * Called for every submitted block that carries data, after the watchdog has
+ * been re-armed for it. Kept apart from the arming because that returns early
+ * when the timeout is 0, and a transmitter muted before the watchdog was
+ * turned off must still come back.
+ */
+void cf_axi_dds_tx_starve_resume(struct cf_axi_dds_state *st)
+{
+	if (st->tx_starved)
+		schedule_work(&st->tx_resume_work);
+}
+EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(cf_axi_dds_tx_starve_resume);
+
 /*
  * Called when a buffer is enabled, before any block has arrived.
  *
@@ -1463,13 +1546,17 @@ EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(cf_axi_dds_tx_starve_arm_first);
 void cf_axi_dds_tx_starve_cancel(struct cf_axi_dds_state *st)
 {
 	cancel_delayed_work_sync(&st->tx_starve_work);
+	cancel_work_sync(&st->tx_resume_work);
 	st->tx_cyclic = false;
+	st->tx_starved = false;
 }
 EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL(cf_axi_dds_tx_starve_cancel);
 
 void cf_axi_dds_tx_starve_init(struct cf_axi_dds_state *st)
 {
 	INIT_DELAYED_WORK(&st->tx_starve_work, cf_axi_dds_tx_starve_fn);
+	INIT_WORK(&st->tx_resume_work, cf_axi_dds_tx_resume_fn);
+	mutex_init(&st->tx_mute_lock);
 	st->tx_starve_ms = CF_AXI_DDS_TX_STARVE_MS_DEFAULT;
 	st->tx_cyclic_ms = 0;		/* off: cyclic is meant to outlive you */
 }
